# Multimodal Fashion Recommendation on H&M Data (VBPR + FashionCLIP)

COMP1818 Artificial Intelligence Applications coursework, University of Greenwich (2026–27).
Author: Fatima Waseem

This project replicates and extends Rippberger & Neidhardt (2025), who used VBPR on the H&M
dataset and found text item features outperformed image features. It compares general-purpose
features (ResNet50 images, SentenceBERT text) with a fashion-specific multimodal model
(FashionCLIP), against random, most-popular, repurchase and tuned BPR baselines.

## Dataset
H&M Personalized Fashion Recommendations (Kaggle):
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/h-and-m-personalized-fashion-recommendations

The raw data (~30 GB including images) is not included in this repository. You must accept the
competition rules on Kaggle to access it.

## Notebooks (run in order)
| Notebook | Purpose | Kaggle link |
|---|---|---|
| 01_praguet.ipynb | EDA, 12-week train / 1-week test split, saves parquet files | [link] |
| 02_baselines_and_evaluation.ipynb | MAP@12 / Recall@12, random, popular, repurchase, BPR + tuning | [link] |
| 03 features.ipynb | SBERT, ResNet50 and FashionCLIP item embeddings | [link] |
| 04_feauture 4 .ipynb | VBPR with each feature set, 3 seeds, repurchase hybrid | [link] |

## How to run
1. Open each notebook on Kaggle (links above) and run them in order 01 → 04.
2. Attach the H&M competition data as an input to every notebook.
3. Attach the outputs of earlier notebooks as inputs to later ones
   (01 → 02, 03, 04; 03 → 04).
4. Enable **GPU** and **Internet** in Settings for notebooks 03 and 04
   (internet is needed to install Cornac and download pretrained models).
5. Saved outputs (parquet splits, embeddings, result CSVs) are available in each Kaggle
   notebook's Output tab, so the results can be checked without rerunning.

Approximate runtimes on a Kaggle T4 GPU: 03 ≈ 30 min, 04 ≈ 2 hours.

## Requirements
See `requirements.txt`. Main libraries: pandas, numpy, pyarrow, cornac, torch, torchvision,
sentence-transformers, transformers, pillow.

## Reproducibility
Random seeds are fixed (42 by default; 7 and 123 for the additional seed runs).
The evaluation uses a time-based split: train 24 Jun – 15 Sep 2020, test 16 – 22 Sep 2020,
with BPR hyperparameters tuned on a validation week (9 – 15 Sep 2020).

## References
- Rippberger, G. & Neidhardt, J. (2025). Comparative Analysis of Fashion Captioning for
  Multimodal Fashion Recommendation. RecSys 2025 Workshop (DaQuaMRec), CEUR-WS.
- He, R. & McAuley, J. (2016). VBPR: Visual Bayesian Personalized Ranking from Implicit Feedback. AAAI.
- Rendle, S. et al. (2009). BPR: Bayesian Personalized Ranking from Implicit Feedback. UAI.
- Chia, P. J. et al. (2022). Contrastive language and vision learning of general fashion concepts
  (FashionCLIP). Scientific Reports.
- Salah, A. et al. (2020). Cornac: A Comparative Framework for Multimodal Recommender Systems. JMLR.
- FashionCLIP model: https://huggingface.co/patrickjohncyh/fashion-clip
